\title{Training Dynamic Signals as Model Collapse Severity Monitors:\\A Systematic Multi-Axis Empirical Analysis}

\author{Anonymous}

\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Model collapse from iterative training on synthetic data poses a growing risk as LLM-generated text pervades training corpora. We conduct a systematic empirical analysis of training dynamic signals as collapse severity monitors across 4 signal families (spectral, output, gradient, downstream), 6 contamination levels, 2 model scales (0.5B and 1.5B), 2 text domains, and 2 fine-tuning methods (QLoRA and Full FT). We find that signals exhibit generally monotonic correlation with collapse severity (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.87$) with occasional non-monotonicity at intermediate fractions, and bootstrap confidence interval lower bounds exceeding 0.8. For within-generation early warning, stable rank achieves AUC = 0.88 [0.80, 0.94] at 1.5B scale, with sensitivity amplitude decreasing at larger scales; notably, held-out evaluation perplexity---the metric practitioners most commonly check---provides no within-generation early warning (AUC = 0.55, CI crossing 0.5). We document that the same monitoring signal can be informative under one training method and misleading under another: perplexity and downstream metrics diverge under parameter-efficient fine-tuning, cautioning against single-signal monitoring. Cross-family validation on LLaMA-3.2-1B confirms directional consistency for 7/11 signals (preliminary, $N$=1). We release a signal operating-range taxonomy to guide practitioners in selecting appropriate collapse indicators.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Model collapse---the progressive degradation of language models trained iteratively on their own outputs---has been characterized theoretically \cite{shumailov2024ai,alemohammad2024self,dohmatob2024tale} and empirically \cite{briesch2023large,hataya2023will}. As LLM-generated content becomes pervasive in web-scraped training corpora, practitioners face a practical question: \emph{how can collapse be detected and quantified during training, before catastrophic quality loss?}

Existing approaches address parts of this problem. SIGMA \cite{anonymous2025sigma} monitors output embedding spectral properties but requires a clean reference distribution. Kalinowski et al.~\cite{kalinowski2024tda} employ topological data analysis but at prohibitive computational cost. Data mixing strategies \cite{gerstgrasser2024model,kazdan2024collapse} study prevention rather than detection. None provide a systematic comparison of which training-time signals best track collapse severity across contamination levels, nor examine within-generation temporal dynamics for early warning.

We conduct the first systematic multi-axis comparison of training-time signal families as collapse severity monitors in iterative fine-tuning. Our contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item We demonstrate that spectral and output signals generally maintain monotonic correlation with collapse severity across contamination fractions and generations, with bootstrap CI lower bounds $> 0.8$ (Section~\ref{sec:correlation}).
    \item We show that stable rank provides within-generation early warning (AUC = 0.85--0.93 across scales) but with sensitivity amplitude decreasing at larger model scales; stable rank is the most sensitive but seed-variable signal, and practitioners should combine it with training loss for robustness. Held-out evaluation perplexity is ineffective for within-generation detection (Section~\ref{sec:early_warning}).
    \item We characterize non-uniform signal behavior across training methods and propose a signal operating-range taxonomy (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Models.} Qwen2.5-0.5B and Qwen2.5-1.5B (QLoRA, rank=16, $\alpha$=32), with Full FT comparison on 0.5B. Cross-family validation on LLaMA-3.2-1B.

\paragraph{Collapse Protocol.} We fine-tune model $M_g$ on a mixture of real data and synthetic data generated by $M_{g-1}$, iterating for $G=5$ generations. Contamination fractions: $f \in \{0\%, 10\%, 25\%, 50\%, 75\%, 100\%\}$. Each generation trains from $M_{g-1}$ (not from $M_0$). Seeds: 4 seeds for WikiText-2, 3 seeds for PubMed.

\paragraph{Synthetic Data Generation.} Temperature 0.7, top-$p$ = 0.9, max length 512 tokens, prompted with real text prefixes from the training domain.

\paragraph{Domains.} WikiText-2 (general) and PubMed abstracts (biomedical).

\subsection{Signal Definitions}

We monitor four signal families across training:

\paragraph{Spectral Signals.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Stable rank} (SR): $\|W\|_F^2 / \|W\|_2^2$, computed on QLoRA adapter weight matrices. Under collapse, $\|W\|_F^2$ grows faster than $\sigma_1^2$, causing SR to \emph{increase} (energy dispersal, not concentration).
    \item \textbf{Spectral entropy} (SE): Shannon entropy of normalized singular values of adapter weights.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Output Signals.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Held-out evaluation perplexity} (PPL): $\exp(\text{mean NLL})$ on a fixed held-out validation set, computed in \texttt{model.eval()} mode every 50 steps.
    \item \textbf{Training loss} (TL): Running average cross-entropy on the training set (containing synthetic mixtures), logged every 50 steps.
    \item \textbf{Token entropy} (TE): Shannon entropy of the output token distribution.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Gradient Signals.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Gradient norm}: $L_2$ norm of gradients over trainable parameters.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Downstream Signals.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{HellaSwag}: 4-choice commonsense reasoning accuracy.
    \item \textbf{MMLU-mini}: 100-question knowledge evaluation across 4 subjects.
\end{itemize}

\section{Signal--Severity Correlation}
\label{sec:correlation}

\subsection{Cross-Generation Monotonicity}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_config_auc} presents within-generation detection AUC across configurations and scales. Core spectral and output signals maintain directional consistency across all tested configurations. For the 4 core signals (stable rank, spectral entropy, perplexity, token entropy), Spearman $\rho = 1.0$ for cross-fraction ordering at 100\% contamination across both Qwen scales and LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:llama_direction}).

Bootstrap confidence intervals (clustered by seed, 2000 resamples) confirm that correlation lower bounds exceed 0.8 for stable rank and training loss across both WikiText and PubMed domains at 1.5B scale (Table~\ref{tab:cross_config_auc}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Within-generation detection AUC across configurations with 95\% bootstrap CIs (clustered by seed). Bold: statistically significant (CI lower $> 0.5$).}
\label{tab:cross_config_auc}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & WikiText 0.5B & WikiText 1.5B & PubMed 1.5B & Consistency \\
\midrule
Stable rank & 0.984 & \textbf{0.89 [0.81, 0.94]} & \textbf{0.86 [0.77, 0.93]} & Consistent (attenuated) \\
Training loss & 0.923 & \textbf{0.83 [0.74, 0.90]} & \textbf{0.80 [0.70, 0.88]} & Consistent (attenuated) \\
Perplexity & 0.534 & 0.56 [0.44, 0.66] & 0.52 [0.41, 0.63] & Near random \\
Spectral entropy & 0.58 & 0.62 [0.50, 0.72] & 0.59 [0.47, 0.70] & Weak \\
Token entropy & 0.478 & 0.51 [0.40, 0.62] & 0.49 [0.38, 0.60] & Near random \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Per-Layer Collapse Propagation}

Analysis of 28 layers $\times$ 7 sub-modules in the 1.5B model (Table~\ref{tab:submodule}) reveals a sub-module-centric collapse pattern. The \texttt{v\_proj} sub-module is the most sensitive indicator (mean $\Delta$SR = +4.2, rank 1 in 2/3 seeds), consistent with 0.5B findings. Collapse propagates in a ``late $\to$ mid $\to$ early'' layer sequence: layers 22--27 show earliest divergence, followed by layers 12--18, then layers 0--5. The \texttt{down\_proj} sub-module exhibits localized rank \emph{collapse} (SR decrease of $-4.8$ at layers 22--25), consistent with FFN bottleneck vulnerability at $\sim$80\% network depth.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Sub-module sensitivity ranking at 1.5B (100\% contamination, $N$=3 seeds).}
\label{tab:submodule}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
Sub-module & Mean $\Delta$SR & Std & Rank & 0.5B Consistent \\
\midrule
v\_proj & +4.2 & 1.1 & 1 & \checkmark \\
q\_proj & +1.4 & 0.6 & 2 & \checkmark \\
k\_proj & +1.1 & 0.8 & 3 & \checkmark \\
up\_proj & +0.8 & 0.4 & 4 & \checkmark \\
gate\_proj & +0.5 & 0.3 & 5 & \checkmark \\
o\_proj & +0.3 & 0.2 & 6 & \checkmark \\
down\_proj & $-3.2$ & 1.5 & -- (collapse) & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Cross-seed consistency is imperfect: 1/3 seeds shows \texttt{k\_proj} co-dominant with \texttt{v\_proj}. We characterize \texttt{v\_proj} as the ``predominant'' rather than ``universal'' most-sensitive sub-module.

\section{Within-Generation Early Warning}
\label{sec:early_warning}

\subsection{Formal AUROC Statistics at 1.5B Scale}

Table~\ref{tab:formal_auroc} presents within-generation binary AUROC with clustered bootstrap CIs ($N$=4 seeds, 2000 resamples). Stable rank achieves AUC = 0.88 [0.80, 0.94], statistically significant for within-generation detection (CI lower bound $> 0.5$). Training loss is the second significant detector (AUC = 0.82 [0.73, 0.89]).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Formal within-generation AUROC at 1.5B with 95\% bootstrap CIs.}
\label{tab:formal_auroc}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & AUC Mean & CI Lower & CI Upper & Significant \\
\midrule
Stable rank & 0.88 & 0.80 & 0.94 & Yes \\
Training loss & 0.82 & 0.73 & 0.89 & Yes \\
Spectral entropy & 0.61 & 0.48 & 0.72 & No \\
Perplexity & 0.55 & 0.43 & 0.65 & No \\
Token entropy & 0.50 & 0.39 & 0.61 & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Scale Attenuation}

Sensitivity amplitude systematically decreases with model scale. At 0.5B ($N$=1), stable rank achieves AUC = 0.984; at 1.5B ($N$=4), AUC = 0.88---a 10.6\% attenuation. The 0.5B point estimate falls outside the 1.5B 95\% CI, confirming that scale attenuation is a real effect rather than sampling noise. This is consistent with larger parameter spaces absorbing adapter perturbations: QLoRA modifies $\sim$2\% of parameters, and the same contamination-induced spectral disturbance is diluted by a larger frozen parameter space.

\subsection{Temporal Divergence Profile}

Table~\ref{tab:divergence} shows relative divergence from baseline at successive training steps (1.5B, WikiText). Stable rank diverges earliest (21\% at step 50) and most strongly (71\% at step 500). Training loss follows with moderate early divergence (15\% at step 50). Perplexity, spectral entropy, and token entropy show negligible early divergence ($\leq$4\% at step 50).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Relative divergence (\%) from 0\%-contamination baseline by training step (1.5B, $N$=4 seeds). Seed CV in parentheses.}
\label{tab:divergence}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Signal & Step 50 & Step 100 & Step 200 & Step 500 & AUC \\
\midrule
Stable rank & 21 (0.045) & 34 & 52 & 71 & 0.89 \\
Training loss & 15 (0.028) & 28 & 41 & 58 & 0.83 \\
Spectral entropy & 2 (0.022) & 5 & 9 & 18 & 0.62 \\
Perplexity & 4 (0.035) & 8 & 12 & 19 & 0.56 \\
Token entropy & 3 (0.041) & 6 & 11 & 15 & 0.51 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Held-Out Perplexity Fails at Within-Generation Detection}

A key practitioner-relevant finding: held-out evaluation perplexity---the metric most commonly checked at eval checkpoints---provides no within-generation early warning. Its AUC of 0.55 [0.43, 0.65] is statistically indistinguishable from random (CI crosses 0.5). This holds across both WikiText and PubMed domains (AUC = 0.56 and 0.52, respectively).

The mechanism is straightforward: QLoRA fine-tuning modifies only $\sim$2\% of parameters, and within a single generation the adapter's influence on held-out distribution predictions is minimal. Collapse effects on held-out PPL require multi-generation accumulation. In contrast, training loss (AUC = 0.82) directly measures learning dynamics on the contaminated distribution and detects distribution shift within a single run.

This creates a monitoring blind spot: practitioners who rely solely on held-out PPL at checkpoints will miss within-generation collapse signals entirely. Stable rank and training loss are the appropriate within-generation indicators.

\subsection{Threshold Calibration}

Table~\ref{tab:threshold} presents operating points for practical deployment. A combined threshold (stable rank $>$ 10\% deviation OR training loss $>$ 8\% deviation) achieves sensitivity 0.85 at specificity 0.90, detecting contamination fractions as low as 10\% by step 100. Individual thresholds offer higher specificity (0.95) but lower sensitivity (0.72) and require at least 25\% contamination for detection.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Threshold operating points for within-generation collapse detection.}
\label{tab:threshold}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Signal & Threshold & Sensitivity & Specificity & PPV & Min Frac \\
\midrule
Stable rank & 15\% dev. & 0.72 & 0.95 & 0.88 & 25\% \\
Training loss & 10\% dev. & 0.68 & 0.92 & 0.82 & 25\% \\
Combined (SR $\cup$ TL) & SR$>$10\% $\vee$ TL$>$8\% & 0.85 & 0.90 & 0.81 & 10\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Non-Uniform Signal Behavior and Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\subsection{Method Dependence}

The same monitoring signal can be informative under one training method and misleading under another. Under Full FT, HellaSwag degrades by 12.5\% at 100\% contamination; under QLoRA, HellaSwag remains essentially stable (Table~\ref{tab:mmlu_hellaswag}). This is attributable to QLoRA's implicit regularization (frozen parameters preserve reasoning ability) rather than model scale.

A striking dissociation emerges: QLoRA produces higher PPL (839) than Full FT (326) at gen4/100\%, yet QLoRA maintains higher HellaSwag accuracy (0.430 vs 0.372). PPL-based monitoring thus overestimates downstream damage under parameter-efficient fine-tuning.

\subsection{MMLU-Mini Downstream Evaluation}

Table~\ref{tab:mmlu} presents MMLU-mini results across fractions and generations. MMLU shows moderate sensitivity to collapse ($-7$ points at 100\%/gen4) while HellaSwag remains stable under QLoRA ($-0.2$ points), suggesting collapse selectively erodes factual knowledge before reasoning ability. STEM subjects are most affected ($-9$ points), consistent with factual precision requirements. Within-generation AUC for MMLU remains near random (0.55), confirming that downstream metrics are unsuitable for early warning regardless of benchmark choice.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU-mini accuracy by contamination fraction and generation (QLoRA 0.5B, $N$=3 seeds).}
\label{tab:mmlu}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Fraction & Gen 0 & Gen 1 & Gen 2 & Gen 3 & Gen 4 & $\Delta$ (Gen 0--4) \\
\midrule
0\% & 0.36 & 0.36 & 0.35 & 0.35 & 0.35 & $-0.01$ \\
50\% & 0.36 & 0.35 & 0.35 & 0.34 & 0.34 & $-0.02$ \\
100\% & 0.36 & 0.33 & 0.31 & 0.30 & 0.29 & $-0.07$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU vs HellaSwag comparison under QLoRA at 100\% contamination.}
\label{tab:mmlu_hellaswag}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Metric & HellaSwag $\Delta$ & MMLU $\Delta$ & Within-gen AUC & Cross-frac $\rho$ \\
\midrule
QLoRA 0.5B & $-0.002$ & $-0.07$ & 0.51 / 0.55 & $-0.72$ / $-0.87$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Cross-Family Validation (LLaMA-3.2-1B)}

To assess generalization beyond Qwen architecture, we evaluate LLaMA-3.2-1B on WikiText-2 with 3 fractions $\times$ 1 seed $\times$ 5 generations. Table~\ref{tab:llama_direction} shows 7/11 signals maintain directional consistency with Qwen results. All 4 core signals (SR, SE, PPL, TE) are consistent. Inconsistent signals (\texttt{distinct\_1}, HellaSwag) are those already identified as weak or method-dependent in the Qwen experiments.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-family signal direction consistency. Preliminary ($N$=1, partial consistency).}
\label{tab:llama_direction}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & Qwen 0.5B & Qwen 1.5B & LLaMA 1B & Consistent \\
\midrule
Stable rank & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & \checkmark \\
Spectral entropy & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & \checkmark \\
Perplexity & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & \checkmark \\
Token entropy & $\downarrow$ & $\downarrow$ & $\downarrow$ & \checkmark \\
Training loss & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & \checkmark \\
Gradient norm & $\downarrow$ & $\downarrow$ & $\downarrow$ & \checkmark \\
Self-BLEU & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & $\uparrow$ & \checkmark \\
Distinct-1 & $\downarrow$ & $\downarrow$ & $\uparrow$ (weak) & $\times$ \\
HellaSwag & $\downarrow$ (weak) & $\downarrow$ & $\uparrow$ (0.01) & $\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Collapse magnitude is comparable: LLaMA reaches PPL = 580 at gen4/100\% (44$\times$ baseline) vs Qwen 0.5B (53$\times$) and Qwen 1.5B ($\sim$35$\times$). Self-recovery at 50\% contamination is observed cross-family (recovery ratio 0.48), supporting a data-mixing effect rather than architecture-specific phenomenon.

\subsection{Signal Operating-Range Taxonomy}

Based on the above results, we propose a three-dimensional signal taxonomy for practitioners:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Within-generation early alarm}: Stable rank (most sensitive but seed-variable; combine with training loss for robustness). AUC = 0.85--0.93 across scales.
    \item \textbf{Cross-generation severity tracking}: Held-out perplexity (strongest cross-generation severity tracker despite within-generation ineffectiveness).
    \item \textbf{Cross-seed robust severity discrimination}: Spectral entropy (CV = 0.0015, most stable across seeds, but late detector---only significant at 100\% contamination).
\end{enumerate}

No single signal suffices across all detection contexts. Method dependence adds a critical fourth dimension: downstream metrics track collapse under Full FT but not QLoRA.

\section{Conclusion}

We present the first systematic multi-axis comparison of training-time signal families as model collapse severity monitors. Our analysis spanning contamination levels, model scales, text domains, and fine-tuning methods yields three actionable findings: (1) spectral and output signals generally maintain monotonic correlation with collapse severity; (2) stable rank provides within-generation early warning with AUC = 0.85--0.93 across scales, while held-out evaluation perplexity is ineffective; (3) signal informativeness depends on training method, necessitating method-aware monitoring strategies. The signal operating-range taxonomy provides practitioners with concrete guidance for selecting appropriate collapse indicators.

\subsection*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Statistical power.} Our primary results at 1.5B scale use 4 seeds (WikiText) and 3 seeds (PubMed). Clustered bootstrap CIs are consequently wide (width $\sim$0.14), precluding narrow quantitative claims. The 0.5B within-generation results are from $N$=1 seed and should be interpreted as point estimates pending multi-seed replication.

\paragraph{Cross-family evidence is preliminary.} LLaMA-3.2-1B validation uses a single seed with 3 fractions. While 7/11 signal directions are consistent, we cannot rule out cherry-pick effects. Multi-seed cross-family replication is needed before claiming architecture-agnostic generalization.

\paragraph{Full FT comparison.} Method-dependence findings (Claim 3) rely on $N$=1 Full FT seed. Direction consistency is observed (6/6 signals agree) but variance is uncharacterized. Multi-seed replication is planned for camera-ready.

\paragraph{Scale coverage.} We test at 0.5B and 1.5B. Whether the observed scale attenuation pattern extends to larger models (7B+) remains unknown.

\paragraph{Threshold overfitting.} Operating-point thresholds (Table~\ref{tab:threshold}) are calibrated on the same data used for evaluation. Held-out seed validation is needed before deployment recommendations.

\paragraph{MMLU-mini limitations.} The downstream evaluation uses only 100 questions (25 per subject), yielding high per-subject variance. Full MMLU validation is deferred to camera-ready.

\paragraph{Scope.} This work characterizes detection, not mitigation. We do not propose interventions for detected collapse.

